{"study_accession":"PRJNA812012","secondary_study_accession":"SRP362516","title":"Circular RNAs to predict clinical outcome after cardiac arrest","description":"Background: Cardiac arrest (CA) represents the third leading cause of death worldwide. Among survivors, severe neurological sequelae are frequent but difficult to predict. Novel prognostic biomarkers would offer clinicians the possibility to deliver personalized healthcare. The potential of small circulating noncoding RNAs (microRNAs) to predict neurological outcome and survival after CA has been reported. Objective: This study aims to identify circulating circular RNAs (circRNAs) associated with clinical outcome after CA. Methods and Results: Methods and Results: Whole blood samples obtained 48h after return of spontaneous circulation from 23 sex-matched survivors and 23 deceased cardiac arrest (CA) patients were enrolled in this study. Whole transcriptome RNA sequencing identified candidate RNAs associated with neurological outcome and survival. Conclusion: We have identified candidate RNAs associated with clinical outcome after CA whose predictive value remains to be confirmed in large populations. Overall design: Whole blood circRNA profiles from 23 cardiac arrest patients with good neurological outcome (CPC1) and 23 patients with bad neurological outcome (CPC5)","pubmed":"36303007","geo_series":"","n_samples":46,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN26437375","study_accession":"PRJNA812012","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"whole_blood_TTM050_CPC5","description":"whole_blood_TTM050_CPC5","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM5929839","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2022-11-03","attributes":[["source_name","whole blood"],["outcome","CPC5"],["subject status","cardiac arrest patient"],["tissue","whole blood"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR18229858","experiment_accession":"SRX14371429","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 20